WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.409 --> 00:00:03.200
JOHN VEIZADES: So my
name is John Veizades.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.230
I'm a product manager
at Google, and I

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:10.870
work a lot on how enterprise
customers connect to our cloud

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:11.950
infrastructure.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:16.450
And today's talk is going
to be exactly about that.

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:18.840
What we're going to be covering
is a little bit about--

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:21.090
and you're going to hear this
throughout the whole week--

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.048
I'm going to talk a little
bit about the Google

00:00:23.048 --> 00:00:27.250
infrastructure, what we--
how you connect to us today

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.020
and then talk a little
bit about how you

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:33.129
connect to us tomorrow or soon.

00:00:33.129 --> 00:00:35.170
Talk a little bit about
architectures, deployment

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:39.220
architectures and how you
can use our cloud networking

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:45.700
to build enterprise networks
that extend out to the cloud.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.790
Something about how we
monitor these systems, what

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.470
we do around monitoring.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:53.830
Some discussion about how you
can get better application

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:55.897
performance when you
come to the cloud.

00:00:55.897 --> 00:00:58.480
And finally, a little bit about
the pricing of all this stuff.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.372
So infrastructure, and
if you go to about half

00:01:05.372 --> 00:01:06.830
of the sessions in
this conference,

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.420
you're going to see a
picture something like this.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.180
And what this is
trying to point out

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:14.619
is where are all the
Cloud data centers?

00:01:14.619 --> 00:01:16.160
When you build an
enterprise network,

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.370
you have Cloud data
centers as well.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.470
Or I should say you
have data centers.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:24.050
This is saying where
Google's data centers are.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.940
And the ones in
green are showing

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:29.520
the ones that are available
today and all the ones in blue

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.170
are data centers that are in
the process of being built out.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.690
The numbers tell
you how many zones

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:37.460
each one of those
data centers has.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.950
And as you can see,
what we're trying to do

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.200
is cover the globe
with data centers

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.910
that are close to customers.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:48.080
Because millisecond closeness
means application performance.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.000
So it's really important to
get that level of connectivity.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.310
The other thing that we
have, and if I take away

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.200
all the Cloud data
centers, what pops out

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.530
is all these little lines,
this is Google's backbone.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:03.020
This is what we have been
spending years and years

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:04.550
on building.

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:08.180
Predominantly it was to
service the applications

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.000
that Google is known
for-- search and YouTube

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.070
and Gmail and all those.

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:18.040
What we're doing with our
Cloud networking infrastructure

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:20.230
is giving you access
to all of this.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:23.110
Now you don't see it because
it's hidden under the covers,

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:26.320
but your application data
runs over this infrastructure.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.720
And it's an infrastructure that
Google continues to build on.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:31.840
So, this is what it
looks like today.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.130
If you see this little--

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:36.190
there's this thing
that says 2018, that's

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.050
a fiber cable that
we're dropping

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:40.660
from somewhere in Portland,
Oregon area to somewhere

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:41.650
in Japan.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:46.210
And we're going to drop in
a little over 100 terabytes

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:51.860
of infrastructure there to
give you more capacity to--

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:54.000
originally it was to
give Google more capacity

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.140
but in short, it's also to give
our customers more capacity

00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.900
as you grow.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.630
So, we're expanding--
in summary,

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:07.080
Google Cloud is an
expanding platform.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:08.410
It's expanding to cover--

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.810
give you a global footprint,
a footprint that's

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:14.830
close to your customers, that
gives you high availability,

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.740
local availability,
the ability to take VMs

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:20.050
and get them closer
to your customers

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:22.870
and continue to drive the
scale that you guys are

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.490
driving us to build.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.420
So today, there are a
number of ways to connect

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.340
to this infrastructure.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.640
The first one is something
what we call direct peering.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.460
This is where you
have a private ASN--

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.740
sorry, a public ASN
and you're connecting

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.342
to our infrastructure at one
of our points of presence.

00:03:43.342 --> 00:03:45.175
I'll go into it where
are points of presence

00:03:45.175 --> 00:03:46.680
are in just a second.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:48.570
This is public peering.

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.570
So when you do that, you get
all of Google's routing table

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:54.030
and you expose your
routing table to us.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.700
Another way to do that is
through a carrier interconnect.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.240
And that's where you go through
carriers that you know and love

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.140
and connect to us through
their infrastructure to us.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:09.340
And finally, if you can't
do either one of these two

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:13.180
or you have a place where you
don't have a lot of capacity,

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.280
you don't need a
lot of capacity,

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:17.810
you can connect to us
using a VPN tunnel.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:19.910
So I'll go into some
detail about that as well.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:27.610
So our Cloud interconnects.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:31.080
First of all, when you go
through those direct peering

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.150
links, you do get
a dedicated link.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.290
That means you have--

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:36.450
how do you say it--

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.790
I wouldn't say enhanced
security but you're just

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:42.150
going over a cross-connect
in a colocation facility.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:44.400
So it's in a protected--
physically protected

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.610
environment.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:50.580
And from a performance
standpoint,

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.230
you're getting
carrier level SLAs,

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:56.180
you're getting locations in
70 locations and 33 countries,

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:59.150
so it's a place where
you can get closer to us

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:01.940
and get better
performance out of it.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:05.270
And finally, a lot of
customers use these methods

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:08.950
for the last reason which is
there's lower egress costs when

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:10.810
you come and meet us
versus when we have

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:14.410
to carry your traffic to you.

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:16.170
And finally with
those systems, you

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:19.830
get access to the whole
set of Google properties.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:23.440
So if you're an enterprise
customer who's rolling out

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.250
G Suite and you want to
have a close connection that

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:27.990
doesn't go over
the internet, these

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.560
are great solutions for you.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.770
Unfortunately, a lot of the
customers, what you really need

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:38.360
is a pri-- extend your
private network into Cloud.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:40.356
And today the way you do that--

00:05:40.356 --> 00:05:45.480
sorry-- is with our
IPsec VPN solution.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.982
So our IPsec VPN solution
is a virtualised solution

00:05:48.982 --> 00:05:49.940
that runs in the Cloud.

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:53.890
It's a scale out VPN
so each tunnel starts

00:05:53.890 --> 00:05:55.540
at about three
gigabits per second

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:59.020
but you can scale that out
based on deploying more

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:01.520
infrastructure on top of that.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:03.820
And what it does
is it lets you take

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:07.690
your RFC1918 space, your
private address space

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.670
and extend it to Cloud.

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:11.590
And we do that with a
couple entities here.

00:06:11.590 --> 00:06:14.880
There's a cloud router
and a VPN gateway.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.920
VPN gateway [INAUDIBLE]
terminates your IPsec sessions

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:22.270
and the Cloud router gives
you the BGP reachability

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:23.900
of everything in your Cloud.

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:26.740
I'm going to make one
comment on Cloud Router

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:29.187
because I'm going to use
this throughout the rest

00:06:29.187 --> 00:06:30.020
of the presentation.

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:34.000
I want you guys to understand
what does the Cloud Router do?

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.330
Yes, it is a BGP endpoint
but that's all it is.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:42.080
It is the control plane of
our routing infrastructure.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:45.430
So when you terminate
a BGP session

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:48.400
on the Cloud Router, what
it does is it programs

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:51.850
the routes into our
SDN infrastructure.

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:54.700
If you-- I don't know if you've
ever read about something

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:56.080
called Andromeda.

00:06:56.080 --> 00:06:59.070
Andromeda is Google's
SDN network architecture.

00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:01.360
There are some great
presentations on it.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:03.380
I'm not going to
cover that here.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:05.380
There is actually some
presentations online that

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:07.660
go into a lot of detail there.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:11.990
But Cloud Router is not the
typical Juniper or Cisco router

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:14.200
that all the packets
go through it.

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:15.520
It just programs the--

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:18.360
it's the control plane that
programs the data plane.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:20.590
So packets don't go through
that so it doesn't--

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:22.320
we don't need a
scale out router.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:25.650
It's a scale out network.

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:27.960
So I just wanted to cover
that before I use it

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.180
in other contexts and
you guys understand

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:32.850
what does the Cloud Router do.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:36.620
When you scale out
VPN too, if you

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.990
need more than three
gigabits per second,

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:43.160
you can use multiple tunnels and
use ECMP across those tunnels

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:45.140
to get greater capacity.

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.360
And we have customers
doing that today

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.030
to drive tens of gigs of
traffic into their Cloud

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:51.207
infrastructure.

00:07:54.070 --> 00:07:59.010
But that seems a
little bit yesterday.

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:03.700
So what we're doing
today is announcing the--

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:06.450
same products that are coming
out later on this year,

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:08.880
actually later on
this quarter, that

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:11.310
will allow you to
extend that using

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:16.940
more traditional networking
architectures other than VPN.

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:20.040
So specifically, we're
announcing two products.

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:21.850
One is called
Private Interconnect.

00:08:21.850 --> 00:08:24.260
Another is called
Partner Interconnect.

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:26.180
Both of these
techniques allow you

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:31.430
to extend your RFC1918 address
space from your premise

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:33.820
to the Cloud.

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:35.770
With the Private
Interconnect, this

00:08:35.770 --> 00:08:39.669
is where you meet us in one
of our colocation facilities

00:08:39.669 --> 00:08:43.299
and you extend a cross-connect
from your routed infrastructure

00:08:43.299 --> 00:08:45.810
to our edge.

00:08:45.810 --> 00:08:50.070
And with Partner Interconnect
is where you go using a carrier.

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:53.520
And they can offer you a sub
rate interface or actually

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:57.750
a full 10 gig interface to
our Cloud infrastructure.

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:02.970
So, Private Interconnect, this
is where an enterprise customer

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:04.880
comes to Google.

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:07.310
And Partner Interconnect is
where an enterprise customer

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:10.790
goes to a carrier and
then comes to Google.

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:15.290
Again, the goal here is
to extend your network

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:18.260
to our network using your
private address space

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:20.820
into Google's-- in your private
address space in the Google

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:21.830
Cloud.

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:24.950
And it doesn't require
the use of any VPN devices

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:29.550
and uses standard routing, as
I'll go into it just a second.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:34.560
So, in a more pictorial view,
with Private Interconnect,

00:09:34.560 --> 00:09:37.050
there's the Google
peering edge and there's

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:38.730
your peering router.

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:41.370
And you're connecting to us
in a colocation facility.

00:09:41.370 --> 00:09:43.770
That means your
network is connecting

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:47.620
to your compute
instances in the Cloud.

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:49.420
And with Partner
Interconnect, you're

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:52.440
coming through a
service provider

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:54.630
from your router to
their infrastructure

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:56.350
into the Google Cloud.

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:57.881
This, as in the
previous diagram,

00:09:57.881 --> 00:09:59.380
these are all layer
two connections.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:01.730
And I'll talk about how
you do the layer three

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:03.660
extensions in just a second.

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:09.757
There are carriers that offer
you a service like a Metro E

00:10:09.757 --> 00:10:11.340
kind of a service
where they offer you

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:13.050
an ethernet connection
and you don't

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:15.490
have to worry about the
routing infrastructure.

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:18.250
So there are two methods
that carriers offer,

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:20.800
one where you have
to deal with BGP

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:23.050
and another one where you
don't have to deal with BGP.

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:27.820
So where do you connect to us?

00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:33.310
Shortly, when we come
out with the product,

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:35.110
you'll be able to
connect to us where

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:37.240
all these green areas are.

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:39.850
And over the course
of 2017, we'll

00:10:39.850 --> 00:10:43.190
be lighting up a lot
of these areas in blue.

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:45.850
These are points of
presence that Google

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:48.310
has across the globe.

00:10:48.310 --> 00:10:51.780
So what is a point of presence?

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:53.670
For instance in
Chicago, we're actually

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:55.410
in two locations in Chicago.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:58.470
We're in CoreSite
and in Equinix.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:02.370
In Frankfurt, we're in four
locations in Frankfurt.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:06.132
So when we say that you're--
we have a metro availability,

00:11:06.132 --> 00:11:08.340
what we're doing is actually
offering you in a number

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:10.150
of connections in that metro.

00:11:10.150 --> 00:11:12.000
And in each one of
those connections,

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:19.500
we offer multiple no shared fate
paths to Google infrastructure.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:23.730
So if for instance, if you
come into CoreSite in Chicago,

00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:26.370
what you'll see is there are two
locations that you can connect

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:28.800
to us in Chicago
in CoreSite, what

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.290
we call a zone 1 and a zone 2.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:33.940
And there is no shared fate
between the two of them.

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:36.120
So if you're building a
highly reliable network,

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:39.250
you probably want to connect
in zone 1 and zone 2.

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:41.520
I'll go to high availability
and architectures

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:46.030
for high availability later
on in this slide deck.

00:11:46.030 --> 00:11:49.090
So in general, it
doesn't matter where

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:50.860
you connect to us in
that metro, you're

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:53.290
connecting into the
Google infrastructure

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:55.090
but in multiple sites
in any one metro.

00:11:57.940 --> 00:12:01.160
So a little-- some comments,
this is all well and good,

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:03.610
but what do I have to do?

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:06.160
Couple of things, this
is all single mode fiber.

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:09.280
We support LACP
for link bonding.

00:12:09.280 --> 00:12:12.250
So we offer 10 gig interfaces.

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:15.460
You can link bond across
multiple 10 gig interfaces

00:12:15.460 --> 00:12:18.940
to go up from 10 gig
up to 80 and even more.

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:21.850
If you go for more, please come
and talk to us because there's

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:24.730
better ways of doing that.

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:27.110
You have to support
a link local address.

00:12:27.110 --> 00:12:29.290
So if you're doing
the private solution,

00:12:29.290 --> 00:12:32.200
you have to have a link
local address on your router.

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:36.980
You have to have a private
ASN that you exchange with us.

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:41.301
802.1q VLAN tagging, and
that's how we'll do traffic

00:12:41.301 --> 00:12:41.800
separation.

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:43.841
I'll go into the details
about traffic separation

00:12:43.841 --> 00:12:45.380
in just a bit.

00:12:45.380 --> 00:12:49.510
And this is all using
iPv4 link local access.

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:55.440
So, a little bit more detail.

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:58.330
So link local address
between your router.

00:12:58.330 --> 00:13:01.030
And the router you connect to
is actually the Cloud Router

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:02.570
that we talked about earlier.

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:05.530
This is where your
virtual connection ends up

00:13:05.530 --> 00:13:09.230
being is on your Cloud
router in your project.

00:13:09.230 --> 00:13:12.700
So in that project, you can
then connect to VM instances

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:14.970
and you're taking your
private address space,

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:17.440
you're going over a
layer 2 connection

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:20.350
to the Google Cloud
infrastructure

00:13:20.350 --> 00:13:23.930
and to your project instances
running in the Google Cloud.

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:31.010
So, if you're in
multiple regions,

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:33.020
you'd have a Cloud Router
in one region, a Cloud

00:13:33.020 --> 00:13:34.880
Router in another
region, and then you'd

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:38.130
have VLAN tags going
between both of them.

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:41.900
So this shows one VLAN
tag going to one region

00:13:41.900 --> 00:13:44.750
and another VLAN tag would
go to another region.

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:48.065
All those instances can talk
to each other over the Google

00:13:48.065 --> 00:13:48.690
Cloud backbone.

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:55.130
That's all well and good,
but let's get better.

00:13:55.130 --> 00:13:57.590
So we're announcing a
feature to Cloud Router

00:13:57.590 --> 00:13:59.260
called global routing.

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:04.790
What global routing does is
why should I-- if I use BGP,

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:08.480
I should be able to advertise
region 2 out of region 1

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:12.080
so now I only have to
deploy one Cloud Router.

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:15.350
That means I only have to peer
with Google in one location

00:14:15.350 --> 00:14:19.620
and now you have access to
our whole infrastructure.

00:14:19.620 --> 00:14:23.140
Remember-- and this is something
that maybe we don't drill

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:24.500
into you guys enough--

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:28.880
is that the network that
you have in a Cloud project

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:33.040
is a network that
spans the globe.

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:34.630
And this is what
I'm showing here, is

00:14:34.630 --> 00:14:38.620
that even though you're
connecting in one region,

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:41.500
you have connectivity
to all the regions

00:14:41.500 --> 00:14:45.156
that you have projects--
your project has VMs in.

00:14:45.156 --> 00:14:48.730
So that means you get access
to that infrastructure

00:14:48.730 --> 00:14:51.900
as showed in earlier slides.

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:55.020
And if you're a multinational
company that wants to,

00:14:55.020 --> 00:15:00.660
for instance, do web serving out
to people all over the globe,

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:04.410
you only really need
one connection to us

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:07.410
and that gives you
access to infrastructure

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:08.160
across the globe.

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:14.880
Now there are customers that
have-- actually most customers

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:15.839
have multiple projects.

00:15:15.839 --> 00:15:18.463
So you might have a dev project
and you might have your release

00:15:18.463 --> 00:15:19.260
project.

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:21.810
You don't want the
two networks to meet.

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:23.580
That would be a very bad thing.

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:26.820
It gives you the chance
of cross-pollination

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:30.060
of information and hackers
can get through one network

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:30.750
into the other.

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:33.660
And most customers want to
keep their production network

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:36.340
separate from your
development network.

00:15:36.340 --> 00:15:39.795
So what you do is you can
have a project with a Cloud

00:15:39.795 --> 00:15:42.580
Router here and another project
with a Cloud Router here.

00:15:42.580 --> 00:15:45.840
There's VLAN tagging that
separates the traffic

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:47.160
between those two projects.

00:15:47.160 --> 00:15:51.000
And on your side, you would do
VRFs to separate the traffic

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:52.840
on your enterprise network.

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:57.550
So this gives you one link into
Google but multiple projects

00:15:57.550 --> 00:15:59.669
using that link and
having traffic separation

00:15:59.669 --> 00:16:00.960
across those multiple projects.

00:16:07.232 --> 00:16:08.690
The other thing--
actually, there's

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:10.670
a talk this afternoon
at 4:00 that's

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:13.610
going to go into a
lot of detail on XPN,

00:16:13.610 --> 00:16:17.060
which is our cross project
networking solution,

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:20.960
also known as a shared VPC.

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:25.460
So, what is a shared
VPC in Google's world?

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:29.150
What happens is that
you have a host project

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:31.210
and you have service projects.

00:16:31.210 --> 00:16:34.940
Your networking for
those service projects

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:36.750
ends up in the host project.

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:39.800
So this is where you virtually
connect to the Google

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:41.810
Cloud in that project.

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:44.210
This is where all your
network resources are,

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:47.000
these are where your
firewall policies can live.

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:50.480
And what that lets you
do is have a separation

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:53.900
of concerns between the folks
that are worried about security

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:58.840
and networking and those that
are worried about development.

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:02.980
So this lets you share a
resource like a network

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:06.819
connection to multiple projects
without those projects really

00:17:06.819 --> 00:17:09.670
needing to know about the
physical infrastructure

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:11.290
of building a network.

00:17:11.290 --> 00:17:13.119
So you can monitor--

00:17:13.119 --> 00:17:15.970
you can have one set of
people monitoring your network

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:17.980
infrastructure and
another set of people

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:21.220
doing all the production
deployments of your VMs

00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:22.630
and all of that.

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:24.520
So you have that
separation of concerns,

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:27.550
you have that separation of
management responsibilities,

00:17:27.550 --> 00:17:29.440
and that extends to
the IM roles that

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:32.501
are associated with these
host and service projects.

00:17:32.501 --> 00:17:34.000
Again, this afternoon,
there's going

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:36.160
to be a presentation
that goes into a lot more

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:38.799
technical detail
about a shared VPC

00:17:38.799 --> 00:17:40.465
and how you can use
it in your projects.

00:17:45.451 --> 00:17:47.450
So as I said, we were
going to talk a little bit

00:17:47.450 --> 00:17:51.080
about high availability.

00:17:51.080 --> 00:17:53.630
When you deploy network
infrastructure anywhere,

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:56.450
you're going to want
to do multiple links

00:17:56.450 --> 00:17:59.210
into our infrastructure.

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:04.160
One of the reasons is that we
can't support an SLA for one

00:18:04.160 --> 00:18:05.647
single connection.

00:18:05.647 --> 00:18:07.480
We do maintenance on
all this infrastructure

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:09.938
and when we do maintenance, we
would take your network out,

00:18:09.938 --> 00:18:11.750
which is not a nice thing.

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:16.010
So, what we suggest doing is
in one colocation facility,

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:17.420
have multiple links.

00:18:17.420 --> 00:18:19.970
That gives you the
ability to have redundancy

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:22.670
and we give you the
benefit of having no shared

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:24.770
fate between those two links.

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:27.890
But in addition
to that, and a lot

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:30.070
of customers want
to go this route,

00:18:30.070 --> 00:18:32.000
is that you want to
do more than that.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:34.310
You want to have
multiple connections

00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:35.630
in multiple regions.

00:18:35.630 --> 00:18:37.250
And the reason you
want to do that

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:40.870
is because you want to deal with
failover, metro level failover.

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:43.370
If your infrastructure
in a metro fails,

00:18:43.370 --> 00:18:46.890
you can failover to
another metro area.

00:18:46.890 --> 00:18:52.030
So, now you have two routers.

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:54.160
What we've also done
in these two routers

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:58.970
is we've built the
ability to use BGP the way

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:00.230
it's meant to be used.

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:02.270
So you can have different
route preferences

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:04.190
in each one of those routers.

00:19:04.190 --> 00:19:07.670
So, let's say this
facility is in London

00:19:07.670 --> 00:19:11.970
and this facility is in the
Bay Area, in San Francisco.

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:14.820
If you get a cable
cut here in London,

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:19.230
your traffic off your network
would end up in San Francisco

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:21.690
because the BGP route
preferences would tell you

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:23.190
that's what you want to do.

00:19:23.190 --> 00:19:25.560
You'd see that there was
a disconnect connectivity

00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:28.020
to your London facility and
you'd route your traffic

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:29.610
to another facility.

00:19:29.610 --> 00:19:31.350
So this gives you--

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:36.630
what we're doing is starting
to extend the Cloud Router,

00:19:36.630 --> 00:19:39.030
the network infrastructure
to look more

00:19:39.030 --> 00:19:41.850
like a traditional
enterprise network.

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:43.410
With the same level
of redundancy,

00:19:43.410 --> 00:19:46.320
the same level of
failover, the same way

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:47.910
of doing all of
these things that you

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:53.550
do in a large
multi-region, multi-country

00:19:53.550 --> 00:19:55.130
enterprise network.

00:19:55.130 --> 00:19:57.210
Oh one other-- one
last thing on this.

00:19:57.210 --> 00:19:59.970
Also when you think about--

00:19:59.970 --> 00:20:02.640
I used to do a lot
of work with telcos.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:05.880
And telcos are all about,
it has to be five nines

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:08.250
and they're starting to
think about six nines.

00:20:08.250 --> 00:20:10.590
So with this kind of
an infrastructure,

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:17.070
you start building that level
of failover, that level of SLA

00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:20.730
by multiplying the number
of connections you have.

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:24.180
So with a description,
if you were

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:27.109
to build a network that
looks something like this,

00:20:27.109 --> 00:20:28.650
you'd be able to
build a network that

00:20:28.650 --> 00:20:31.250
had a five nines reliability.

00:20:37.730 --> 00:20:39.400
A number of customers
have come to us

00:20:39.400 --> 00:20:42.940
and said, yeah that private
address space is really good.

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:46.570
But what I really want to do
is I have some public address

00:20:46.570 --> 00:20:49.030
space and I would like to
back up that public address

00:20:49.030 --> 00:20:52.510
space with infrastructure
in the Cloud.

00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:56.200
And there's a way
to do that in GCP.

00:20:56.200 --> 00:20:58.720
The first thing you would
do is at the VM level,

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:01.990
you'd attach an IP address
to each one of those VMs.

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:03.820
So you're taking that
public IP address

00:21:03.820 --> 00:21:06.280
and attaching it at
the operating system

00:21:06.280 --> 00:21:08.470
level of the VM.

00:21:08.470 --> 00:21:10.900
The second thing
you'd do is you'd--

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:13.660
with another feature
that's coming out shortly--

00:21:13.660 --> 00:21:17.620
is you'd be able to change the
route that is being advertised.

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:22.390
Today, the Cloud Router
advertises in a very static way

00:21:22.390 --> 00:21:24.190
what routes are available.

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:27.010
We're adding a feature
called flexible routing that

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:30.840
will allow you to specify which
routes you would advertise.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:32.840
A reason you'd want to
do this is for instance,

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:35.200
you may have a
production network

00:21:35.200 --> 00:21:38.350
that you're not ready to go
and expose to the whole world,

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:40.810
that you want to
have it all ready.

00:21:40.810 --> 00:21:42.700
So you want the routing
infrastructure ready.

00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:45.080
You'll be able to flip
a bit on that network

00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:47.320
and then it'll start to be
advertised off the Cloud

00:21:47.320 --> 00:21:48.100
Router.

00:21:48.100 --> 00:21:54.490
It's a way too predictably
release routes out to your

00:21:54.490 --> 00:21:56.667
on premise infrastructure.

00:21:56.667 --> 00:21:58.250
So that's a feature
that's coming out.

00:21:58.250 --> 00:22:01.850
So, in Cloud Router,
you'd add the prefixes

00:22:01.850 --> 00:22:06.170
for those public IPs that you
want to advertise over BGP.

00:22:06.170 --> 00:22:11.150
Then you'd add a static
route from the private IP.

00:22:11.150 --> 00:22:15.020
Because these VM instances
will have a Google private IP

00:22:15.020 --> 00:22:17.660
and you need to know how to
get the reverse traffic back.

00:22:17.660 --> 00:22:21.880
So you'd put a static route,
taking that private IP

00:22:21.880 --> 00:22:24.160
to the public IP of the VM.

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:27.700
Sorry, from the public IP
to the private IP of the VM.

00:22:27.700 --> 00:22:31.320
And finally, you'd
set up your router

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:34.610
so that advertises as those
to the rest of the world.

00:22:34.610 --> 00:22:38.705
And that is a method
for getting public IPs

00:22:38.705 --> 00:22:43.130
into the Google infrastructure
that are your own public IPs.

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:45.560
Because today, the only
way you can use a public IP

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:47.480
is if you take something
out of our range.

00:22:52.190 --> 00:22:55.280
So, for monitoring,
so now we've given you

00:22:55.280 --> 00:22:59.000
a link to our
infrastructure and you've

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:00.920
decided to provision
10 gig and you

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:04.280
have no idea how much of
that link you're using.

00:23:04.280 --> 00:23:08.440
So there'll be-- this
is a mock up of the user

00:23:08.440 --> 00:23:11.530
interface that you'd see
in the Cloud Console.

00:23:11.530 --> 00:23:13.810
And we'll do things
like link utilization,

00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:16.240
drops, items like that.

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:18.140
We'll also give you--

00:23:18.140 --> 00:23:19.970
suggest that you
get a ping endpoint

00:23:19.970 --> 00:23:24.410
so you can measure round trip
times and monitor that as well.

00:23:24.410 --> 00:23:28.240
And all of these statistics
will be available in Stackdriver

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:30.610
so you can actually
extract them and put them

00:23:30.610 --> 00:23:32.096
into your own console.

00:23:32.096 --> 00:23:33.970
So you don't have to go
to the Google Console

00:23:33.970 --> 00:23:35.080
to get all this data.

00:23:35.080 --> 00:23:37.205
You can actually get it
out and use it on your own.

00:23:41.290 --> 00:23:43.760
Another thing we're changing--

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:47.190
we've done some fundamental
changes in how you interconnect

00:23:47.190 --> 00:23:49.060
to the Google infrastructure.

00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:51.700
And doing fundamental
changes means you probably

00:23:51.700 --> 00:23:54.340
fundamentally want to
change the way we describe

00:23:54.340 --> 00:23:56.620
that in the Cloud Console.

00:23:56.620 --> 00:24:00.190
So today, we're
interconnected, as you see,

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:01.420
there's something called VPN.

00:24:01.420 --> 00:24:04.600
That's going to change so
it would be interconnect.

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:08.260
And that will subsume VPN
and private and partner

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:09.070
interconnect.

00:24:09.070 --> 00:24:12.280
So you'd have one place to
go in our user interface

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:16.000
to control those interconnect
methods into the Cloud.

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:25.350
So, now you've moved your
applications to the Cloud.

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:27.990
You've connected to us
in a variety of areas.

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:29.890
It's still different.

00:24:29.890 --> 00:24:31.800
It's not your data
center, right?

00:24:31.800 --> 00:24:35.700
It's not the rack of machines
that's down the hall.

00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:40.170
And what we found is that
customers have a problem,

00:24:40.170 --> 00:24:42.720
their applications have
problems with this.

00:24:42.720 --> 00:24:44.730
Because we've changed--
fundamentally,

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:47.970
we've changed some
of the network, some

00:24:47.970 --> 00:24:50.070
of the basic
networking parameters

00:24:50.070 --> 00:24:52.407
that applications are used to.

00:24:52.407 --> 00:24:54.240
And what that big change
we've done is we've

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:59.320
changed the latency between
your front end, the application,

00:24:59.320 --> 00:25:00.540
and the back end.

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:02.850
Whether it be a database
server or whether it

00:25:02.850 --> 00:25:07.480
be some other REST API that
that application is using.

00:25:07.480 --> 00:25:09.780
And those changes
mean that you may

00:25:09.780 --> 00:25:13.070
want to think about changing
the way the application behaves.

00:25:13.070 --> 00:25:15.630
So I'm going to go into
some details about how

00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:18.060
you can change the
application to make sure

00:25:18.060 --> 00:25:20.970
that you're getting the
most of our network.

00:25:20.970 --> 00:25:23.520
And it really has to do about
something called the bandwidth

00:25:23.520 --> 00:25:24.810
delay product.

00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:29.100
And let me go into a little
bit about why-- what this is.

00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:31.910
So if you look at typical Cloud
latencies, and I'm throwing--

00:25:31.910 --> 00:25:35.730
showing you three Google
regions that we have--

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:40.200
and the average latency to
each one of those regions

00:25:40.200 --> 00:25:42.250
from our pop locations.

00:25:42.250 --> 00:25:45.120
So if you have an instance
running in our US Central

00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:48.030
region and you connect
to us at the closest

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:50.700
place you can connect to
that US Central region,

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:52.500
you're still about
10 milliseconds away

00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:54.110
from those VMs.

00:25:54.110 --> 00:25:57.900
In US East, it's about
10 to 25 milliseconds.

00:25:57.900 --> 00:26:00.820
And finally in Europe,
because it's a smaller place,

00:26:00.820 --> 00:26:03.660
it's about five to
seven milliseconds.

00:26:03.660 --> 00:26:04.780
That's different.

00:26:04.780 --> 00:26:05.760
And the difference is.

00:26:05.760 --> 00:26:09.307
Profound because
what you'll see,

00:26:09.307 --> 00:26:11.890
and now I'm going to go into a
little bit of details about how

00:26:11.890 --> 00:26:15.820
TCP works, if you think
about it, what TCP does

00:26:15.820 --> 00:26:18.910
is it sends a bunch
of data down the pipe.

00:26:18.910 --> 00:26:22.790
And it sends a Windows
worth of data down the pipe.

00:26:22.790 --> 00:26:24.620
When you're in the
same data center,

00:26:24.620 --> 00:26:26.180
your application
and the back end

00:26:26.180 --> 00:26:28.650
are running in the
same data center.

00:26:28.650 --> 00:26:32.640
The time it takes to send
that is fairly quick.

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:34.336
Well, it's the time
to take-- regardless

00:26:34.336 --> 00:26:36.210
of where you're at, the
time it takes to send

00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:38.100
it is still about the same.

00:26:38.100 --> 00:26:40.410
What's very different
is how long it

00:26:40.410 --> 00:26:42.690
takes to acknowledge that data.

00:26:42.690 --> 00:26:45.330
Because your data
goes down the pipe,

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:48.210
has to get to its destination
before an acknowledgement can

00:26:48.210 --> 00:26:49.990
come back.

00:26:49.990 --> 00:26:53.310
And on a network
that is less than a

00:26:53.310 --> 00:26:57.660
millisecond in delay,
this time it's very short.

00:26:57.660 --> 00:27:02.310
But as you get to even short
delays like seven milliseconds,

00:27:02.310 --> 00:27:06.510
if you use the default
settings in a TCP kernel,

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:10.590
you're going to get
really bad performance.

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:13.230
And I get a lot of calls
from customers saying,

00:27:13.230 --> 00:27:14.140
I connected to you.

00:27:14.140 --> 00:27:15.940
I have these big pipes
to you and I still

00:27:15.940 --> 00:27:17.410
get really lousy performance.

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:18.970
Why?

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:20.780
And they're
forgetting one thing,

00:27:20.780 --> 00:27:25.510
is I need to change the TCP
behavior to take advantage

00:27:25.510 --> 00:27:27.400
of the--

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:30.070
well not take advantage,
to remediate the issues

00:27:30.070 --> 00:27:32.320
you have with this
milliseconds of latency

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:34.900
that you've added
to your application.

00:27:34.900 --> 00:27:37.990
And the way you do
that is by increasing

00:27:37.990 --> 00:27:41.600
the advertised window
in your TCP session.

00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:43.990
So rather than advertising
in the default window,

00:27:43.990 --> 00:27:47.230
you want to extend that
window so that you can send

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:49.450
more data to the remote site.

00:27:49.450 --> 00:27:52.000
Now this doesn't help
applications that--

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:53.835
especially HTTP
applications that

00:27:53.835 --> 00:27:56.980
are doing small
transactions all the time,

00:27:56.980 --> 00:27:58.450
but it really
affects applications

00:27:58.450 --> 00:28:00.620
that are trying to
move lots of data.

00:28:00.620 --> 00:28:02.920
And that's where
more often than not,

00:28:02.920 --> 00:28:05.470
you complain about slowness.

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:09.320
You're doing an upload
into a GCS Cloud Bucket.

00:28:09.320 --> 00:28:12.100
You're taking the
movie you just finished

00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:14.080
rendering and downloading it.

00:28:14.080 --> 00:28:16.150
Doing those kinds of
things can take a long time

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:21.070
if you don't take into account
the delay bandwidth product.

00:28:21.070 --> 00:28:23.800
And the way to do that is by
increasing the window size.

00:28:23.800 --> 00:28:26.020
And when you increase the
window size, what happens

00:28:26.020 --> 00:28:29.590
is that you send more data
and you acknowledge more data

00:28:29.590 --> 00:28:32.830
at the same time, meaning
you can keep the pipe full.

00:28:32.830 --> 00:28:36.370
So if you're getting
10 gigabit pipes to us,

00:28:36.370 --> 00:28:39.610
you really want to keep
the 10 gigabits full,

00:28:39.610 --> 00:28:44.270
especially if you're doing
these large data transfers.

00:28:44.270 --> 00:28:47.590
So, just some notes on--

00:28:47.590 --> 00:28:52.080
one last note on this subject
is that many of the TCP kernels

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:54.040
are already set up this way.

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:57.880
And the reason I bring this up
is because if you do see that,

00:28:57.880 --> 00:28:59.330
it's a good way
to go check this.

00:28:59.330 --> 00:29:03.520
And if you do a Wikipedia search
on delay bandwidth product,

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:06.580
there's tons of articles that
go into how you'd go and look

00:29:06.580 --> 00:29:10.140
at these features and how you
calculate all these things.

00:29:13.990 --> 00:29:18.880
